+1.1 For $p$ near $q$ put $\Delta_h(t):=\phi_X^t(p+h)-\phi_X^t(p)$. The integral equations from [F1] and the one-variable fundamental theorem applied to $\theta\mapsto X(\phi_X^s(p)+\theta\Delta_h(s))$ give $$\Delta_h(t)=h+\int_0^t A_h(s)\Delta_h(s)\,ds,\quad A_h(s):=\int_0^1DX(\phi_X^s(p)+\theta\Delta_h(s))\,d\theta.$$ The segments lie in $K$ after shrinking the initial ball and time interval. The local Lipschitz estimate in [F1] gives $\sup_t\|\Delta_h(t)\|=O(\|h\|)$. Uniform continuity of $DX$ on $K$ from [F4] makes $A_h(s)$ converge uniformly to $DX(\phi_X^s(p))$ as $h\to0$. [F1, F2, F3, F4]
 
 2.1 Let $\mathcal I=[-\delta,\delta]$ and let $\mathcal C_1=C(\mathcal I,M_m(\mathbb R))$ with the supremum operator norm. This space is complete: for a supremum-Cauchy sequence $(J_k)$, each matrix entry at each $s$ is Cauchy because $|A_{ij}|\le\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}}$; [F5] applied to the $m^2$ entries gives a pointwise matrix limit $J(s)$, and the uniform Cauchy estimate passed to each entry gives uniform convergence of all entries. Each limit entry is continuous by the $\varepsilon/3$ argument for uniform limits of continuous functions. The finite-coordinate bound $\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le\sum_{i,j}|A_{ij}|$ then gives uniform operator-norm convergence, so $J\in\mathcal C_1$. The space is nonempty because it contains the constant zero matrix curve. Put $A_0(s):=DX(\phi_X^s(p))$ and define $T(J)(t):=I_m+\int_0^t A_0(s)J(s)\,ds$. It maps $\mathcal C_1$ into itself and is a contraction with constant at most $L\delta<1$. By [F6], it has a unique fixed point $J$. Define $R_h(t):=\Delta_h(t)-J(t)h$. Subtracting the integral equations and writing the integrand difference as $(A_h-A_0)\Delta_h+A_0R_h$ gives $\|R_h\|_\infty\le L\delta\|R_h\|_\infty+\delta\|A_h-A_0\|_\infty\|\Delta_h\|_\infty=o(\|h\|)$, by step 1.1 and uniform continuity of $DX$ on $K$. Thus $D_q\phi_X^t(p)=J(t,p)$. The continuous integrand in the equation for $J$ has average over $[t,t+h]$ tending to its value at $t$: the quotient error is at most $\sup_{|s-t|\le|h|}\|DX(\phi_X^s(p))J(s,p)-DX(\phi_X^t(p))J(t,p)\|$, which tends to zero. Thus $\partial_tJ=DX(\phi_X^t(p))J$ and $J(0,p)=I_m$. [step 1.1, F1, F3, F4, F5, F6, algebra]
